.TH FORK 2
.SH NAME
fork \- spawn new process
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "fork( )"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fork
is the only way new processes are created.
The new process' core
image is a copy of that of the caller of
.BR fork .
The only distinction is the
fact that the value returned in the old (parent) process contains the process
ID of the new (child) process, while the value returned in the child is
0.
Process ID's range from 1 to 30,000.
This process ID is used by
.BR wait (2)
and
.BR kill (2).
.PP
Files open before the
.B fork
are shared, and have a common read-write
pointer.
In particular, this is the way that standard input and output files
are passed and also how pipes are set up.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
wait(2), exec(2), kill(2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Returns \-1 and fails to create a process if: the user is not super-user, or
the system's process table is full.
Only the super-user can take the last
process-table slot.
.SH ASSEMBLER
(fork = 2.)
.TP
PDP-11:
.nf
sys fork
(new process return)
(old process return, R0 = new process ID)
.fi
The return locations in the old and new process differ by one
work.
The C-bit is set in the old process if a new process could
not be created.
.TP
8086:
.nf
BX = 2
int 0Xf1
AX = 0 for child, child process ID for parent
.fi
.sp
